This page needs a significant but small wording improvement.

The current summary correctly identifies the fundamental problem: very high transaction volume creates substantial synchronous Oracle insert pressure, IO contention, session pile-up and redo/undo pressure. 

Change 1 — Avoid saying the architecture is inherently impossible

Current wording essentially says the symptoms are:

> “expected and inevitable consequences of this architecture”



I would soften this.

Use:

> “These symptoms are consistent with the current synchronous logging design and are expected to become increasingly difficult to control as transaction volumes grow.”



This is more defensible.

Change 2 — Add the target solution

Add a second paragraph:

> The recommended direction is a phased transition from synchronous Oracle-centric logging to an asynchronous event-driven logging architecture. IBM MQ will remain the enterprise messaging layer, while Kafka will provide durable, horizontally scalable event buffering and fan-out to Oracle logging, Data Lake/analytics and future consumers.



Change 3 — Add one important phrase

Add:

> Oracle remains available for workloads that require relational/operational persistence but is removed from the synchronous logging critical path.



That is the key architectural message.


